--TEST--
Core\File::read and Core\File::write refuse the same path under their own capability, and a refusal is catchable — being granted a root to read is never permission to write in it, and a denial leaves the program running — ADR 0118 §§ 2, 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing configured this run, so nothing is granted: ADR 0118 § 1's deny-by-
// default is what both doors answer with here, and the interesting half is
// *which* capability each one names. The path is identical in both calls on
// purpose -- what differs between the two lines below is the verb, so a door
// that checked one capability for both effects would print the same name twice.
string $path = "report.txt";

try {
    string $content = Core\File::read($path);
    echo "read: allowed, ", $content, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "read: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\File::write($path, "anything at all");
    echo "write: allowed\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "write: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// ADR 0118 § 5: a denial is the world refusing a call, not ADR 0020's `FATAL`.
// Both clauses above caught, so this line prints -- which is the whole of what
// separates a capability refusal from a limit breach, and it is asserted here
// rather than in a comment because a `FATAL` would take the line with it.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
read: Core\File::read needs the capability `fs.read` for report.txt, which is not granted
write: Core\File::write needs the capability `fs.write` for report.txt, which is not granted
still running
